.TH WB\-PLAN 1 "" "watchbill"
.SH NAME
wb\-plan \- take a plan in, and list, show, edit, accept or remove it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwb\fR plan add [<name>] (\-F <file> | \-m <text>... | \-\-from <adapter> <number>)
            [\-\-source <address>] [\-\-level <level>] [\-\-hash\-only] [\-q | \-\-quiet]
            \-F is \-\-file and \-m is \-\-message
            or list [\-q | \-\-quiet]
            or show <name> [\-q | \-\-quiet]
            or edit <name> [\-q | \-\-quiet]
            or accept <name> [\-\-no\-commit | \-n] [\-q | \-\-quiet]
            or remove <name> [\-q | \-\-quiet]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The plans of the jobs, kept in git (docs/revision.md, point 15). wb plan add takes a job in. Its text is given in one of three ways: \-F <file>, where \-F \- reads standard input, a pipe or a here\-document; \-m <text>, which may be given more than once and is joined and cleaned up of whitespace as git commit \-m does it; or \-\-from <adapter> <number>, which runs the pattern issue_text of watchbill.yml where its tracker: is the adapter, and names the job <adapter>/<number>. \-F is \-\-file and \-m is \-\-message, as in git. A file is read only where it is a plain file of 1 MiB or less, and a text that is empty or not UTF\-8 is refused. The text is stored as a git blob under its hash, and the name of the job is the ref refs/watchbill/<name>: a commit whose tree holds job.yml and the text under sources/<hash>. job.yml holds the first name, every name and, for each source, its hash, \-\-source <address> or none, the level, the git identity of the person and the time. With no name the job is named by the first 12 hex digits of the hash. A name may hold / (github/54); a name that git check\-ref\-format refuses, such as one with :, is refused. Other text under a name that is there adds a source, as a new commit after the one before; the same text again changes nothing; text that is already a job under another name gives that job this name too. Every name of the job is moved to its new commit together, or none is. \-\-level <level> is a level of the organisation's file no higher than the repository's, which is taken when none is given. \-\-hash\-only keeps the hash and the source and not the text, where the organisation's file allows it with jobs: hash_only: allowed. It says what it will write first, asks nothing of the network unless \-\-from runs a pattern, and only queues: it runs no prompt and starts no session. wb plan add ends with 0 when the job is taken in or nothing changes, 1 when it is refused, 2 when it was used wrongly or the repository is not set up. wb plan list prints each plan once, by its first name, one to a line, and nothing else; a plan removed is not listed, and a ref under refs/watchbill/ that is not a job is passed over and said on standard error. wb plan show <name> prints the plan's names, each source with its hash, where it came from, whether its text is kept, its level, who added it and when, then the issue's file .watchbill/issues/<name>.yml, read where wb status reads it (the issue's branch, else the working tree), and says which, and its state: not planned, not accepted, not accepted (edited after the acceptance), not accepted (a source added after the acceptance), not accepted (planned again after the acceptance), or accepted by whom and when; the planner's stamps are read whole by issue_comment_bodies. A source or a [stamp] planner of the same second as the acceptance or later voids it, and no prompt of the issue is handed out until wb plan accept again; a stamp that a [revert] took back counts for nothing. wb plan edit <name> opens the issue's file in the editor git would use (GIT_EDITOR, core.editor, VISUAL, EDITOR), on the issue's branch, from the file as HEAD has it, and commits it alone with the message Edit the issue's file (<name>); where nothing changed it commits nothing, and where the editor fails, the file no longer holds its form or names another issue, it commits nothing and leaves the edit. wb plan accept <name> [\-\-no\-commit | \-n] accepts the issue's file as wb accept does. wb plan remove <name> adds a commit with the marker removed after the plan's, moves every name of it there, and rewrites nothing; the name stays taken. It is refused while a branch of any of its names holds work the default branch does not, or this machine keeps a view of it with work not taken back. A name with / has no issue's file until point 15, and show, edit, accept and remove of a name that is no plan are refused. Ends with 0 when it is done or nothing changes, 1 when it is refused, 2 when it was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.PP
\-q (\-\-quiet) leaves out the lines that confirm what a subcommand wrote and the lines that say there is nothing to do, and not what it says before it writes, a refusal, that an acceptance holds no longer, or what list and show print.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-F <file>, \-\-file <file>
Read the text from the file, a plain file of 1 MiB or less, or from standard input where the file is \-, as for a pipe.
.TP
.B \-m <text>, \-\-message <text>
The text in words; given more than once, each is a paragraph, cleaned up of whitespace as git commit \-m does it.
.TP
.B \-\-from <adapter> <number>
Read the text by the pattern issue_text of watchbill.yml, where its tracker: is the adapter, and name the job <adapter>/<number>.
.TP
.B \-\-source <address>
Where the text came from, kept in job.yml; none when it is not given.
.TP
.B \-\-level <level>
The level of the job: one of the organisation's levels, no higher than the repository's.
.TP
.B \-\-hash\-only
Keep the hash of the text and its source, not the text, where the organisation's file has jobs: hash_only: allowed.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-commit, \-n
Of wb plan accept: only write the acceptance and say the commit, as wb accept \-\-no\-commit does.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Leave out the lines that only confirm what was done or say there was nothing to do; never a refusal, a warning, what is said before a write, or a command the person has to run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
It is done, or nothing changes: the job is taken in, the list or the plan is printed, the edit is committed or changed nothing, the plan is accepted or removed.
.TP
.B 1
It is refused: for add, a name git refuses, the name of a plan removed, a level above the repository's, \-\-hash\-only not allowed, no organisation's file, or a text that is empty, over 1 MiB or not UTF\-8; for show, edit, accept and remove, a name that is no plan or a plan removed; for edit, a checkout off the issue's branch, a file not as HEAD has it, an editor that failed, or a file that no longer holds its form; for accept, what wb accept refuses; for remove, a role's work on the plan that is not done.
.TP
.B 2
It was used wrongly, no adapter is set up for \-\-from, or the repository is not set up.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/watchbill/organisation.yml
The levels, and whether \-\-hash\-only is allowed.
